      },
      "trials": [
        {
          "trial": 1,
          "host": {
            "durationMs": 51718,
            "turns": 14,
            "costUsd": 0.4272,
            "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
            "answer": "`npx protoboard check` now reports 0 errors; both faults were in `src/quadcopter.uhd.ts` and nothing under `parts/` or `src/harnesses/` was touched. I could not run the fault-scenario script (the command was denied), so the scenarios are unconfirmed after the change.\n\n## The two faults\n\n1. **GNSS lead: RX and TX swapped at the FC pads.** The `gnss_pads` link soldered the GNSS RX wire to the FC's RX1 pad and the TX wire to TX1, so neither side could hear the other. I crossed them back (GNSS RX on TX1, GNSS TX on RX1), as the lead's own usage note specifies. This also cleared the `HARNESS_ROUTE_STALE` warning on the GNSS lead.\n\n2. **DJI O4 power: fed from the battery instead of the FC's 10 V BEC.** A `video_power` link ran the O4's VCC straight from the pack (18\u201325.2 V) against its 3.7\u201313.2 V input, and the `dji_fc` plug link had been restricted to pins 2\u20136, leaving the cable's 10 V conductor disconnected. I removed `video_power` and restored `dji_fc` to a full six-pin plug, so the model again derives 10 V BEC \u2192 O4 VCC through the 3-in-1 cable (10 W of the BEC's 25 W).\n\n## What is left\n\n- **Warnings:** four remain, none tied to these faults: three for vendor CAD files the catalog does not distribute for the M9N, and the motor peak current (171.88 A) exceeding the pack's 110 A continuous rating while staying within its 220 A burst rating.\n- **The bench itself:** if the model was really updated to match the bench, the physical quad may carry both faults. Check the O4 lead before powering it, since pack voltage on VCC is well over its 13.2 V limit.\n- **Generated outputs:** I did not regenerate any (firmware CLI, BOM); `check` reports nothing stale.",
            "status": "completed"
          },
