            "model": "claude-opus-5-5",
            "answer": "`npx protoboard check` now reports 0 errors after two fixes in `src/quadcopter.uhd.ts`. I could not run the fault scenarios (the command was denied in this session), so those are unverified.\n\n**Fault 1 \u2014 GNSS lead soldered TX-to-TX / RX-to-RX.** The `gnss_pads` composition put the lead's RX wire on the FC's RX1 pad and its TX wire on TX1, so neither side could hear the other. I swapped them so GNSS RX lands on TX1 and GNSS TX on RX1, as the lead's own usage note says. This also cleared the stale-route warning on the GNSS lead, with no CAD regeneration needed.\n\n**Fault 2 \u2014 O4 powered straight from the battery.** A `video_power` link fed the O4's VCC from the 6S pack (18\u201325.2 V) against the O4's 3.7\u201313.2 V input, and the `dji_fc` link had been restricted to positions 2\u20136, leaving the 10 V wire off the FC's DJI socket. I removed the battery link and restored the cable as a full straight-through connection, so the O4 is fed from the FC's 10 V BEC through the 3-in-1 cable.\n\nThe four remaining warnings are untouched: three are vendor CAD files for the M9N that the catalog does not distribute, and one is the motors' combined peak current exceeding the pack's continuous rating but staying within its burst rating.\n\nIf the bench really was repaired the way the model described, the quad itself needs rework: the GNSS TX/RX wires swapped at the FC pads, and the O4 power lead moved off battery voltage, which may already have damaged the air unit.",
