            "answer": "`npx protoboard check` now reports 0 errors after two fixes in `src/quadcopter.uhd.ts`. The model had two wiring faults, both introduced by the bench update.\n\n1. **O4 power lead on battery voltage.** The model had the O4's VCC fed straight from the battery (18\u201325.2 V on 6S) while the air unit accepts 3.7\u201313.2 V, and the DJI socket link had been cut down to pins 2\u20136 so the FC's 10 V pin was left out. I removed the `video_power` battery link and restored the full 6-pin `dji_fc` link, so VCC comes from the FC's 10 V BEC through the 3-in-1 cable again.\n\n2. **GNSS UART not crossed.** The re-soldered lead had its RX wire on the FC's RX1 pad and its TX wire on TX1, so neither side could hear the other. I swapped the two in the `gnss_pads` link so GNSS RX lands on TX1 and GNSS TX on RX1, as the lead's own usage note specifies.\n\nThe GNSS lead's stale-route warning cleared with the second fix. The four remaining warnings were there before the fixes and are untouched: three vendor CAD files for the Matek M9N that the catalog does not distribute, and the motor peak current (171.88 A) exceeding the pack's continuous rating but not its burst rating.\n\nIf the physical quad really is wired the way the model was, the bench needs correcting too: the O4's power lead must come off battery voltage before it is powered, and the GNSS RX/TX wires need swapping on the FC pads.",
